Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

### `[signal]` void QAction::triggered(bool *checked* \\= false)

This signal is emitted when an action is activated by the user; for example, when the
user clicks a menu option, toolbar button, or presses an action's shortcut key
combination, or when
[trigger](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qaction.html#trigger)() was called.
Notably, it is *not* emitted when
[setChecked](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qaction.html#checked-prop)() or
[toggle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qaction.html#toggle)() is called.

If the action is checkable, *checked* is true if the action is checked, or false if the
action is unchecked.

**See also**
[QAction::activate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qaction.html#activate)(),
[QAction::toggled](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qaction.html#toggled)(), and
[checked](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qaction.html#checked-prop).

Source: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qaction.html#triggered"""
