### `[static]` void QCursor::setPos([QScreen](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qscreen.html) \\**screen*, int *x*, int *y*)

Moves the cursor (hot spot) of the *screen* to the global screen position (*x*, *y*).

You can call
[QWidget::mapToGlobal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#mapToGlobal)() to
translate widget coordinates to global screen coordinates.

**Note:** Calling this function results in changing the cursor position through the
windowing system. The windowing system will typically respond by sending mouse events to
the application's window. This means that the usage of this function should be avoided
in unit tests and everywhere where fake mouse events are being injected via
QWindowSystemInterface because the windowing system's mouse state (with regards to
buttons for example) may not match the state in the application-generated events.

**Note:** On platforms where there is no windowing system or cursors are not available,
this function may do nothing.

**See also** [pos](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcursor.html#pos)(),
[QWidget::mapFromGlobal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#mapFromGlobal)(),
and
[QWidget::mapToGlobal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#mapToGlobal)().

