## Detailed Description

QOpenGLWindow is an enhanced [QWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html)
that allows easily creating windows that perform OpenGL rendering using an API that is
compatible with [QOpenGLWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwidget.html)
and is similar to the legacy QGLWidget. Unlike
[QOpenGLWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwidget.html), QOpenGLWindow
has no dependency on the widgets module and offers better performance.

A typical application will subclass QOpenGLWindow and reimplement the following virtual
functions:

- [initializeGL](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwindow.html#initializeGL)() to perform OpenGL resource initialization
- [resizeGL](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwindow.html#resizeGL)() to set up the transformation matrices and other window size dependent resources
- [paintGL](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwindow.html#paintGL)() to issue OpenGL commands or draw using [QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html)

To schedule a repaint, call the
[update](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevicewindow.html#update-2)()
function. Note that this will not immediately result in a call to
[paintGL](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwindow.html#paintGL)(). Calling
[update](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevicewindow.html#update-2)() multiple
times in a row will not change the behavior in any way.

This is a slot so it can be connected to a
[QTimer::timeout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtimer.html#timeout)() signal to
perform animation. Note however that in the modern OpenGL world it is a much better
choice to rely on synchronization to the vertical refresh rate of the display. See
[setSwapInterval()](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsurfaceformat.html#setSwapInterval)
on a description of the swap interval. With a swap interval of `1`, which is the case on
most systems by default, the
[swapBuffers()](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglcontext.html#swapBuffers)
call, that is executed internally by QOpenGLWindow after each repaint, will block and
wait for vsync. This means that whenever the swap is done, an update can be scheduled
again by calling
[update](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevicewindow.html#update-2)(), without
relying on timers.

To request a specific configuration for the context, use
[setFormat](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html#setFormat)() like for any
other [QWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html). This allows, among
others, requesting a given OpenGL version and profile, or enabling depth and stencil
buffers.

Unlike [QWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html), QOpenGLWindow allows
opening a painter on itself and perform
[QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html)-based drawing.

QOpenGLWindow supports multiple update behaviors. The default, `NoPartialUpdate` is
equivalent to a regular, OpenGL-based
[QWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html) or the legacy QGLWidget. In
contrast, `PartialUpdateBlit` and `PartialUpdateBlend` are more in line with
[QOpenGLWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwidget.html)'s way of working,
where there is always an extra, dedicated framebuffer object present. These modes allow,
by sacrificing some performance, redrawing only a smaller area on each paint and having
the rest of the content preserved from of the previous frame. This is useful for
applications than render incrementally using
[QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html), because this way they do
not have to redraw the entire window content on each
