class QPaintDevice:
    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

A paint device is an abstraction of a two-dimensional space that can be drawn on using a
[QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html). Its default coordinate
system has its origin located at the top-left position. X increases to the right and Y
increases downwards. The unit is one pixel.

The drawing capabilities of QPaintDevice are currently implemented by the
[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html),
[QImage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qimage.html),
[QPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpixmap.html), QGLPixelBuffer,
[QPicture](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpicture.html), and
[QPrinter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qprinter.html) subclasses.

To implement support for a new backend, you must derive from QPaintDevice and
reimplement the virtual
[paintEngine](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#paintEngine)()
function to tell [QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html) which
paint engine should be used to draw on this particular device. Note that you also must
create a corresponding paint engine to be able to draw on the device, i.e derive from
[QPaintEngine](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintengine.html) and reimplement its
virtual functions.

**Warning:** Qt requires that a
[QGuiApplication](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qguiapplication.html) object exists
before any paint devices can be created. Paint devices access window system resources,
and these resources are not initialized before an application object is created.

The QPaintDevice class provides several functions returning the various device metrics:
The [depth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#depth)() function
returns its bit depth (number of bit planes). The
[height](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#height)() function returns
its height in default coordinate system units (e.g. pixels for
[QPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpixmap.html) and
[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html)) while
[heightMM](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#heightMM)() returns the
height of the device in millimeters. Similiarily, the
[width](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#width)() and
[widthMM](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#widthMM)() functions
return the width of the device in default coordinate system units and in millimeters,
respectively. Alternatively, the protected
[metric](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#metric)() function can be
used to retrieve the metric information by specifying the desired
[PaintDeviceMetric](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#PaintDeviceMetric-enum)
as argument.

The [logicalDpiX](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#logicalDpiX)()
and [logicalDpiY](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#logicalDpiY)()
functions return the horizontal and vertical resolution of the device in dots per inch.
The [physicalDpiX](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#physicalDpiX)()
and [physicalDpiY](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#physicalDpiY)()
functions also return the resolution of the device in dots per inch, but note that if
the logical and physical resolution differ, the corresponding
[QPaintEngine](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintengine.html) must handle the
mapping. Finally, the
[colorCount](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#colorCount)() function
returns the number of different colors available for the paint device.

Source: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html"""

