class QValidator:
    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

The class itself is abstract. Two subclasses,
[QIntValidator](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qintvalidator.html) and
[QDoubleValidator](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdoublevalidator.html), provide
basic numeric-range checking, and
[QRegExpValidator](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexpvalidator.html) provides
general checking using a custom regular expression.

If the built-in validators aren't sufficient, you can subclass QValidator. The class has
two virtual functions:
[validate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#validate)() and
[fixup](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#fixup)().

[validate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#validate)() must be
implemented by every subclass. It returns
[Invalid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum),
[Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) or
[Acceptable](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) depending on
whether its argument is valid (for the subclass's definition of valid).

These three states require some explanation. An
[Invalid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) string is
*clearly* invalid.
[Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) is less
obvious: the concept of validity is difficult to apply when the string is incomplete
(still being edited). QValidator defines
[Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) as the
property of a string that is neither clearly invalid nor acceptable as a final result.
[Acceptable](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) means that
the string is acceptable as a final result. One might say that any string that is a
plausible intermediate state during entry of an
[Acceptable](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) string is
[Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum).

Here are some examples:

- For a line edit that accepts integers from 10 to 1000 inclusive, 42 and 123 are [Acceptable](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum), the empty string, 5, or 1234 are [Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum), and "asdf" and 10114 is [Invalid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum).
- For an editable combobox that accepts URLs, any well-formed URL is [Acceptable](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum), "http://example.com/," is [Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) (it might be a cut and paste action that accidentally took in a comma at the end), the empty string is [Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) (the user might select and delete all of the text in preparation for entering a new URL) and "http:///./" is [Invalid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum).
- For a spin box that accepts lengths, "11cm" and "1in" are [Acceptable](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum), "11" and the empty string are [Intermediate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum), and "http://example.com" and "hour" are [Invalid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum).

[fixup](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#fixup)() is provided for
validators that can repair some user errors. The default implementation does nothing.
[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html), for example, will call
[fixup](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#fixup)() if the user presses
Enter (or Return) and the content is not currently valid. This allows the
[fixup](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#fixup)() function the
opportunity of performing some magic to make an
[Invalid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum) string
[Acceptable](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#State-enum).

A validator has a locale, set with
[setLocale](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html#setLocale)(). It is
typically used to parse localized data. For example,
[QIntValidator](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qintvalidator.html) and
[QDoubleValidator](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdoublevalidator.html) use it to
parse localized representations of integers and doubles.

QValidator is typically used with
[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html),
[QSpinBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qspinbox.html) and
[QComboBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcombobox.html).

Source: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html"""

