    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### baseSize : [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the base size of the widget\n\nThe base size is used to calculate a proper widget size if the widget defines\n[sizeIncrement](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeIncrement-prop)().\n\nBy default, for a newly-created widget, this property contains a size with zero width\nand height.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **baseSize**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setBaseSize**(*const QSize \\&*) |\n| void | **[setBaseSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setBaseSize-1)**(int *basew*, int *baseh*) |\n\n**See also**\n[setSizeIncrement](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeIncrement-prop)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#baseSize'
    childrenRect: QRect
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### childrenRect : const [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)\n\nThis property holds the bounding rectangle of the widget's children\n\nHidden children are excluded.\n\nBy default, for a widget with no children, this property contains a rectangle with zero\nwidth and height located at the origin.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QRect | **childrenRect**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[childrenRegion](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#childrenRegion-prop)()\nand [geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#childrenRect"
    childrenRegion: QRegion
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### childrenRegion : const [QRegion](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregion.html)\n\nThis property holds the combined region occupied by the widget's children\n\nHidden children are excluded.\n\nBy default, for a widget with no children, this property contains an empty region.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QRegion | **childrenRegion**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[childrenRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#childrenRect-prop)(),\n[geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop)(), and\n[mask](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#mask)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#childrenRegion"
    contextMenuPolicy: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### contextMenuPolicy : [Qt::ContextMenuPolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#ContextMenuPolicy-enum)\n\nhow the widget shows a context menu\n\nThe default value of this property is\n[Qt::DefaultContextMenu](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#ContextMenuPolicy-enum),\nwhich means the\n[contextMenuEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#contextMenuEvent)()\nhandler is called. Other values are\n[Qt::NoContextMenu](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#ContextMenuPolicy-enum),\n[Qt::PreventContextMenu](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#ContextMenuPolicy-enum),\n[Qt::ActionsContextMenu](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#ContextMenuPolicy-enum),\nand\n[Qt::CustomContextMenu](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#ContextMenuPolicy-enum).\nWith\n[Qt::CustomContextMenu](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#ContextMenuPolicy-enum),\nthe signal\n[customContextMenuRequested](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#customContextMenuRequested)()\nis emitted.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| Qt::ContextMenuPolicy | **contextMenuPolicy**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setContextMenuPolicy**(Qt::ContextMenuPolicy *policy*) |\n\n**See also**\n[contextMenuEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#contextMenuEvent)(),\n[customContextMenuRequested](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#customContextMenuRequested)(),\nand [actions](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#actions)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#contextMenuPolicy'
    cursor: QCursor
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### cursor : [QCursor](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcursor.html)\n\nThis property holds the cursor shape for this widget\n\nThe mouse cursor will assume this shape when it's over this widget. See the [list of\npredefined cursor objects](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#CursorShape-enum)\nfor a range of useful shapes.\n\nAn editor widget might use an I-beam cursor:\n\n```\nsetCursor(Qt::IBeamCursor);\n```\n\nIf no cursor has been set, or after a call to unsetCursor(), the parent's cursor is\nused.\n\nBy default, this property contains a cursor with the\n[Qt::ArrowCursor](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#CursorShape-enum) shape.\n\nSome underlying window implementations will reset the cursor if it leaves a widget even\nif the mouse is grabbed. If you want to have a cursor set for all widgets, even when\noutside the window, consider\n[QGuiApplication::setOverrideCursor](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qguiapplication.html#setOverrideCursor)().\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QCursor | **cursor**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setCursor**(*const QCursor \\&*) |\n| void | **unsetCursor**() |\n\n**See also**\n[QGuiApplication::setOverrideCursor](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qguiapplication.html#setOverrideCursor)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#cursor"
    enabled: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### enabled : bool\n\nThis property holds whether the widget is enabled\n\nIn general an enabled widget handles keyboard and mouse events; a disabled widget does\nnot. An exception is made with\n[QAbstractButton](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qabstractbutton.html).\n\nSome widgets display themselves differently when they are disabled. For example a button\nmight draw its label grayed out. If your widget needs to know when it becomes enabled or\ndisabled, you can use the\n[changeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#changeEvent)() with type\n[QEvent::EnabledChange](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#Type-enum).\n\nDisabling a widget implicitly disables all its children. Enabling respectively enables\nall child widgets unless they have been explicitly disabled. It it not possible to\nexplicitly enable a child widget which is not a window while its parent widget remains\ndisabled.\n\nBy default, this property is `true`.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isEnabled**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setEnabled**(*bool*) |\n\n**See also**\n[isEnabledTo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isEnabledTo)(),\n[QKeyEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qkeyevent.html),\n[QMouseEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qmouseevent.html), and\n[changeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#changeEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#enabled'
    focus: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### focus : const bool\n\nThis property holds whether this widget (or its focus proxy) has the keyboard input\nfocus\n\nBy default, this property is `false`.\n\n**Note:** Obtaining the value of this property for a widget is effectively equivalent to\nchecking whether\n[QApplication::focusWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#focusWidget)()\nrefers to the widget.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **hasFocus**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [setFocus](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setFocus-1)(),\n[clearFocus](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#clearFocus)(),\n[setFocusPolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#focusPolicy-prop)(),\nand\n[QApplication::focusWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#focusWidget)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#focus'
    focusPolicy: Any
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### focusPolicy : [Qt::FocusPolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#FocusPolicy-enum)\n\nThis property holds the way the widget accepts keyboard focus\n\nThe policy is\n[Qt::TabFocus](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#FocusPolicy-enum) if the\nwidget accepts keyboard focus by tabbing,\n[Qt::ClickFocus](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#FocusPolicy-enum) if the\nwidget accepts focus by clicking,\n[Qt::StrongFocus](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#FocusPolicy-enum) if it\naccepts both, and\n[Qt::NoFocus](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#FocusPolicy-enum) (the default)\nif it does not accept focus at all.\n\nYou must enable keyboard focus for a widget if it processes keyboard events. This is\nnormally done from the widget's constructor. For instance, the\n[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html) constructor calls\nsetFocusPolicy([Qt::StrongFocus](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#FocusPolicy-enum)).\n\nIf the widget has a focus proxy, then the focus policy will be propagated to it.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| Qt::FocusPolicy | **focusPolicy**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setFocusPolicy**(Qt::FocusPolicy *policy*) |\n\n**See also**\n[focusInEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#focusInEvent)(),\n[focusOutEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#focusOutEvent)(),\n[keyPressEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#keyPressEvent)(),\n[keyReleaseEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#keyReleaseEvent)(),\nand [enabled](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#enabled-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#focusPolicy"
    font: QFont
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### font : [QFont](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfont.html)\n\nThis property holds the font currently set for the widget\n\nThis property describes the widget's requested font. The font is used by the widget's\nstyle when rendering standard components, and is available as a means to ensure that\ncustom widgets can maintain consistency with the native platform's look and feel. It's\ncommon that different platforms, or different styles, define different fonts for an\napplication.\n\nWhen you assign a new font to a widget, the properties from this font are combined with\nthe widget's default font to form the widget's final font. You can call\n[fontInfo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#fontInfo)() to get a copy of\nthe widget's final font. The final font is also used to initialize\n[QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html)'s font.\n\nThe default depends on the system environment.\n[QApplication](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html) maintains a\nsystem/theme font which serves as a default for all widgets. There may also be special\nfont defaults for certain types of widgets. You can also define default fonts for\nwidgets yourself by passing a custom font and the name of a widget to\n[QApplication::setFont](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#setFont)().\nFinally, the font is matched against Qt's font database to find the best match.\n\n[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html) propagates explicit font\nproperties from parent to child. If you change a specific property on a font and assign\nthat font to a widget, that property will propagate to all the widget's children,\noverriding any system defaults for that property. Note that fonts by default don't\npropagate to windows (see\n[isWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isWindow)()) unless the\n[Qt::WA\\_WindowPropagation](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WidgetAttribute-enum)\nattribute is enabled.\n\n[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html)'s font propagation is similar\nto its palette propagation.\n\nThe current style, which is used to render the content of all standard Qt widgets, is\nfree to choose to use the widget font, or in some cases, to ignore it (partially, or\ncompletely). In particular, certain styles like GTK style, Mac style, and Windows Vista\nstyle, apply special modifications to the widget font to match the platform's native\nlook and feel. Because of this, assigning properties to a widget's font is not\nguaranteed to change the appearance of the widget. Instead, you may choose to apply a\n[styleSheet](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html).\n\n**Note:** If [Qt Style Sheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html) are\nused on the same widget as setFont(), style sheets will take precedence if the settings\nconflict.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| const QFont \\& | **font**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setFont**(*const QFont \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [fontInfo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#fontInfo)() and\n[fontMetrics](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#fontMetrics)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#font"
    frameGeometry: QRect
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### frameGeometry : const [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)\n\ngeometry of the widget relative to its parent including any window frame\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QRect | **frameGeometry**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop)(),\n[x](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#x-prop)(),\n[y](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#y-prop)(), and\n[pos](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#pos-prop)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#frameGeometry"
    frameSize: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### frameSize : const [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the size of the widget including any window frame\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **frameSize**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#frameSize"
    fullScreen: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### fullScreen : const bool\n\nThis property holds whether the widget is shown in full screen mode\n\nA widget in full screen mode occupies the whole screen area and does not display window\ndecorations, such as a title bar.\n\nBy default, this property is `false`.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isFullScreen**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[windowState](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowState)(),\n[minimized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimized-prop), and\n[maximized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximized-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#fullScreen'
    geometry: QRect
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### geometry : [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)\n\nThis property holds the geometry of the widget relative to its parent and excluding the\nwindow frame\n\nWhen changing the geometry, the widget, if visible, receives a move event\n([moveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#moveEvent)()) and/or a\nresize event\n([resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)())\nimmediately. If the widget is not currently visible, it is guaranteed to receive\nappropriate events before it is shown.\n\nThe size component is adjusted if it lies outside the range defined by\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)() and\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop)().\n\n**Warning:** Calling setGeometry() inside\n[resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)() or\n[moveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#moveEvent)() can lead to\ninfinite recursion.\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| const QRect \\& | **geometry**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **[setGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setGeometry-1)**(int *x*, int *y*, int *w*, int *h*) |\n| void | **setGeometry**(*const QRect \\&*) |\n\n**See also**\n[frameGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#frameGeometry-prop)(),\n[rect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#rect-prop)(),\n[move](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#pos-prop)(),\n[resize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop)(),\n[moveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#moveEvent)(),\n[resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)(),\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)(), and\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry"
    height: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### height : const int\n\nThis property holds the height of the widget excluding any window frame\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\n**Note:** Do not use this function to find the height of a screen on a [multiple screen\ndesktop](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdesktopwidget.html). Read [this\nnote](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdesktopwidget.html#screen-geometry) for\ndetails.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **height**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop),\n[width](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#width-prop), and\n[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#height"
    inputMethodHints: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### inputMethodHints : [Qt::InputMethodHints](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#InputMethodHint-enum)\n\nWhat input method specific hints the widget has.\n\nThis is only relevant for input widgets. It is used by the input method to retrieve\nhints as to how the input method should operate. For example, if the\n[Qt::ImhFormattedNumbersOnly](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#InputMethodHint-enum)\nflag is set, the input method may change its visual components to reflect that only\nnumbers can be entered.\n\n**Warning:** Some widgets require certain flags in order to work as intended. To set a\nflag, do `w->setInputMethodHints(w->inputMethodHints()|f)` instead of\n`w->setInputMethodHints(f)`.\n\n**Note:** The flags are only hints, so the particular input method implementation is\nfree to ignore them. If you want to be sure that a certain type of characters are\nentered, you should also set a\n[QValidator](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvalidator.html) on the widget.\n\nThe default value is\n[Qt::ImhNone](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#InputMethodHint-enum).\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.6.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| Qt::InputMethodHints | **inputMethodHints**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setInputMethodHints**(Qt::InputMethodHints *hints*) |\n\n**See also**\n[inputMethodQuery](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#inputMethodQuery)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#inputMethodHints'
    isActiveWindow: bool
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### isActiveWindow : const bool\n\nThis property holds whether this widget's window is the active window\n\nThe active window is the window that contains the widget that has keyboard focus (The\nwindow may still have focus if it has no widgets or none of its widgets accepts keyboard\nfocus).\n\nWhen popup windows are visible, this property is `true` for both the active window *and*\nfor the popup.\n\nBy default, this property is `false`.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isActiveWindow**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[activateWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#activateWindow)() and\n[QApplication::activeWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#activeWindow)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isActiveWindow"
    layoutDirection: Any
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### layoutDirection : [Qt::LayoutDirection](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#LayoutDirection-enum)\n\nThis property holds the layout direction for this widget.\n\n**Note:** This method no longer affects text layout direction since Qt 4.7.\n\nBy default, this property is set to\n[Qt::LeftToRight](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#LayoutDirection-enum).\n\nWhen the layout direction is set on a widget, it will propagate to the widget's\nchildren, but not to a child that is a window and not to a child for which\nsetLayoutDirection() has been explicitly called. Also, child widgets added *after*\nsetLayoutDirection() has been called for the parent do not inherit the parent's layout\ndirection.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| Qt::LayoutDirection | **layoutDirection**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setLayoutDirection**(Qt::LayoutDirection *direction*) |\n| void | **unsetLayoutDirection**() |\n\n**See also**\n[QApplication::layoutDirection](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qguiapplication.html#layoutDirection-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#layoutDirection"
    locale: QLocale
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### locale : [QLocale](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlocale.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's locale\n\nAs long as no special locale has been set, this is either the parent's locale or (if\nthis widget is a top level widget), the default locale.\n\nIf the widget displays dates or numbers, these should be formatted using the widget's\nlocale.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.3.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QLocale | **locale**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setLocale**(const QLocale \\&*locale*) |\n| void | **unsetLocale**() |\n\n**See also** [QLocale](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlocale.html) and\n[QLocale::setDefault](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlocale.html#setDefault)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#locale"
    maximized: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### maximized : const bool\n\nThis property holds whether this widget is maximized\n\nThis property is only relevant for windows.\n\n**Note:** Due to limitations on some window systems, this does not always report the\nexpected results (e.g., if the user on X11 maximizes the window via the window manager,\nQt has no way of distinguishing this from any other resize). This is expected to improve\nas window manager protocols evolve.\n\nBy default, this property is `false`.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isMaximized**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[windowState](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowState)(),\n[showMaximized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showMaximized)(),\n[visible](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#visible-prop),\n[show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)(),\n[hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)(),\n[showNormal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showNormal)(), and\n[minimized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimized-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximized'
    maximumHeight: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### maximumHeight : int\n\nThis property holds the widget's maximum height in pixels\n\nThis property corresponds to the height held by the\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop)\nproperty.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value of 16777215\\.\n\n**Note:** The definition of the `QWIDGETSIZE_MAX` macro limits the maximum size of\nwidgets.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **maximumHeight**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMaximumHeight**(int *maxh*) |\n\n**See also**\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop) and\n[maximumWidth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumWidth-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumHeight"
    maximumSize: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### maximumSize : [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's maximum size in pixels\n\nThe widget cannot be resized to a larger size than the maximum widget size.\n\nBy default, this property contains a size in which both width and height have values of\n16777215\\.\n\n**Note:** The definition of the `QWIDGETSIZE_MAX` macro limits the maximum size of\nwidgets.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **maximumSize**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMaximumSize**(*const QSize \\&*) |\n| void | **[setMaximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setMaximumSize-1)**(int *maxw*, int *maxh*) |\n\n**See also**\n[maximumWidth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumWidth-prop),\n[maximumHeight](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumHeight-prop),\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop), and\n[sizeIncrement](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeIncrement-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize"
    maximumWidth: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### maximumWidth : int\n\nThis property holds the widget's maximum width in pixels\n\nThis property corresponds to the width held by the\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop)\nproperty.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value of 16777215\\.\n\n**Note:** The definition of the `QWIDGETSIZE_MAX` macro limits the maximum size of\nwidgets.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **maximumWidth**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMaximumWidth**(int *maxw*) |\n\n**See also**\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop) and\n[maximumHeight](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumHeight-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumWidth"
    minimized: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### minimized : const bool\n\nThis property holds whether this widget is minimized (iconified)\n\nThis property is only relevant for windows.\n\nBy default, this property is `false`.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isMinimized**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[showMinimized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showMinimized)(),\n[visible](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#visible-prop),\n[show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)(),\n[hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)(),\n[showNormal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showNormal)(), and\n[maximized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximized-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimized'
    minimumHeight: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### minimumHeight : int\n\nThis property holds the widget's minimum height in pixels\n\nThis property corresponds to the height held by the\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)\nproperty.\n\nBy default, this property has a value of 0\\.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **minimumHeight**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMinimumHeight**(int *minh*) |\n\n**See also**\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop) and\n[minimumWidth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumWidth-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumHeight"
    minimumSize: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### minimumSize : [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's minimum size\n\nThe widget cannot be resized to a smaller size than the minimum widget size. The\nwidget's size is forced to the minimum size if the current size is smaller.\n\nThe minimum size set by this function will override the minimum size defined by\n[QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html). In order to unset the\nminimum size, use a value of `QSize(0, 0)`.\n\nBy default, this property contains a size with zero width and height.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **minimumSize**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMinimumSize**(*const QSize \\&*) |\n| void | **[setMinimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setMinimumSize-1)**(int *minw*, int *minh*) |\n\n**See also**\n[minimumWidth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumWidth-prop),\n[minimumHeight](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumHeight-prop),\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop), and\n[sizeIncrement](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeIncrement-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize"
    minimumSizeHint: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### minimumSizeHint : const [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the recommended minimum size for the widget\n\nIf the value of this property is an invalid size, no minimum size is recommended.\n\nThe default implementation of minimumSizeHint() returns an invalid size if there is no\nlayout for this widget, and returns the layout's minimum size otherwise. Most built-in\nwidgets reimplement minimumSizeHint().\n\n[QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html) will never resize a widget to\na size smaller than the minimum size hint unless\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)() is set\nor the size policy is set to QSizePolicy::Ignore. If\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)() is\nset, the minimum size hint will be ignored.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| virtual QSize | **minimumSizeHint**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [QSize::isValid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html#isValid)(),\n[resize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop)(),\n[setMinimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)(),\nand [sizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizePolicy-prop)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSizeHint"
    minimumWidth: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### minimumWidth : int\n\nThis property holds the widget's minimum width in pixels\n\nThis property corresponds to the width held by the\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)\nproperty.\n\nBy default, this property has a value of 0\\.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **minimumWidth**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMinimumWidth**(int *minw*) |\n\n**See also**\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop) and\n[minimumHeight](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumHeight-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumWidth"
    modal: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### modal : const bool\n\nThis property holds whether the widget is a modal widget\n\nThis property only makes sense for windows. A modal widget prevents widgets in all other\nwindows from getting any input.\n\nBy default, this property is `false`.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isModal**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [isWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isWindow)(),\n[windowModality](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowModality-prop),\nand [QDialog](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdialog.html).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#modal'
    mouseTracking: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### mouseTracking : bool\n\nThis property holds whether mouse tracking is enabled for the widget\n\nIf mouse tracking is disabled (the default), the widget only receives mouse move events\nwhen at least one mouse button is pressed while the mouse is being moved.\n\nIf mouse tracking is enabled, the widget receives mouse move events even if no buttons\nare pressed.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **hasMouseTracking**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMouseTracking**(bool *enable*) |\n\n**See also**\n[mouseMoveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#mouseMoveEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#mouseTracking'
    normalGeometry: QRect
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### normalGeometry : const [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)\n\nThis property holds the geometry of the widget as it will appear when shown as a normal\n(not maximized or full screen) top-level widget\n\nFor child widgets this property always holds an empty rectangle.\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty rectangle.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QRect | **normalGeometry**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[QWidget::windowState](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowState)()\nand [QWidget::geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#normalGeometry'
    objectName: Any
    palette: QPalette
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### palette : [QPalette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpalette.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget\'s palette\n\nThis property describes the widget\'s palette. The palette is used by the widget\'s style\nwhen rendering standard components, and is available as a means to ensure that custom\nwidgets can maintain consistency with the native platform\'s look and feel. It\'s common\nthat different platforms, or different styles, have different palettes.\n\nWhen you assign a new palette to a widget, the color roles from this palette are\ncombined with the widget\'s default palette to form the widget\'s final palette. The\npalette entry for the widget\'s background role is used to fill the widget\'s background\n(see\n[QWidget::autoFillBackground](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#autoFillBackground-prop)),\nand the foreground role initializes\n[QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html)\'s pen.\n\nThe default depends on the system environment.\n[QApplication](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html) maintains a\nsystem/theme palette which serves as a default for all widgets. There may also be\nspecial palette defaults for certain types of widgets (e.g., on Windows Vista, all\nclasses that derive from [QMenuBar](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qmenubar.html)\nhave a special default palette). You can also define default palettes for widgets\nyourself by passing a custom palette and the name of a widget to\n[QApplication::setPalette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#setPalette)().\nFinally, the style always has the option of polishing the palette as it\'s assigned (see\n[QStyle::polish](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html#polish)()).\n\n[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html) propagates explicit palette\nroles from parent to child. If you assign a brush or color to a specific role on a\npalette and assign that palette to a widget, that role will propagate to all the\nwidget\'s children, overriding any system defaults for that role. Note that palettes by\ndefault don\'t propagate to windows (see\n[isWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isWindow)()) unless the\n[Qt::WA\\_WindowPropagation](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WidgetAttribute-enum)\nattribute is enabled.\n\n[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html)\'s palette propagation is\nsimilar to its font propagation.\n\nThe current style, which is used to render the content of all standard Qt widgets, is\nfree to choose colors and brushes from the widget palette, or in some cases, to ignore\nthe palette (partially, or completely). In particular, certain styles like GTK style,\nMac style, and Windows Vista style, depend on third party APIs to render the content of\nwidgets, and these styles typically do not follow the palette. Because of this,\nassigning roles to a widget\'s palette is not guaranteed to change the appearance of the\nwidget. Instead, you may choose to apply a\n[styleSheet](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html).\n\n**Warning:** Do not use this function in conjunction with [Qt Style\nSheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html). When using style sheets,\nthe palette of a widget can be customized using the "color", "background-color",\n"selection-color", "selection-background-color" and "alternate-background-color".\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| const QPalette \\& | **palette**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setPalette**(*const QPalette \\&*) |\n\n**See also**\n[QGuiApplication::palette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qguiapplication.html#palette)(),\n[QWidget::font](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#font-prop)(), and [Qt\nStyle Sheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#palette'
    pos: QPoint
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### pos : [QPoint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpoint.html)\n\nThis property holds the position of the widget within its parent widget\n\nIf the widget is a window, the position is that of the widget on the desktop, including\nits frame.\n\nWhen changing the position, the widget, if visible, receives a move event\n([moveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#moveEvent)()) immediately.\nIf the widget is not currently visible, it is guaranteed to receive an event before it\nis shown.\n\nBy default, this property contains a position that refers to the origin.\n\n**Warning:** Calling move() or\n[setGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop)() inside\n[moveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#moveEvent)() can lead to\ninfinite recursion.\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QPoint | **pos**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **[move](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#move-1)**(int *x*, int *y*) |\n| void | **move**(*const QPoint \\&*) |\n\n**See also**\n[frameGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#frameGeometry-prop),\n[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop),\n[x](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#x-prop)(), and\n[y](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#y-prop)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#pos'
    rect: QRect
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### rect : const [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)\n\nThis property holds the internal geometry of the widget excluding any window frame\n\nThe rect property equals [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)(0, 0,\n[width](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#width-prop)(),\n[height](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#height-prop)()).\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QRect | **rect**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#rect"
    size: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### size : [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the size of the widget excluding any window frame\n\nIf the widget is visible when it is being resized, it receives a resize event\n([resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)())\nimmediately. If the widget is not currently visible, it is guaranteed to receive an\nevent before it is shown.\n\nThe size is adjusted if it lies outside the range defined by\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)() and\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop)().\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Warning:** Calling resize() or\n[setGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop)() inside\n[resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)() can lead to\ninfinite recursion.\n\n**Note:** Setting the size to `QSize(0, 0)` will cause the widget to not appear on\nscreen. This also applies to windows.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **size**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **[resize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resize-1)**(int *w*, int *h*) |\n| void | **resize**(*const QSize \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [pos](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#pos-prop),\n[geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop),\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop),\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop),\n[resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)(), and\n[adjustSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#adjustSize)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size"
    sizeHint: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### sizeHint : const [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the recommended size for the widget\n\nIf the value of this property is an invalid size, no size is recommended.\n\nThe default implementation of sizeHint() returns an invalid size if there is no layout\nfor this widget, and returns the layout's preferred size otherwise.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| virtual QSize | **sizeHint**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [QSize::isValid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html#isValid)(),\n[minimumSizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSizeHint-prop)(),\n[sizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizePolicy-prop)(),\n[setMinimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)(),\nand [updateGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#updateGeometry)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint"
    sizeIncrement: QSize
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### sizeIncrement : [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the size increment of the widget\n\nWhen the user resizes the window, the size will move in steps of\nsizeIncrement().[width](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#width-prop)()\npixels horizontally and\nsizeIncrement.[height](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#height-prop)()\npixels vertically, with\n[baseSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#baseSize-prop)() as the\nbasis. Preferred widget sizes are for non-negative integers *i* and *j*:\n\n```\nwidth = baseSize().width() + i * sizeIncrement().width();\nheight = baseSize().height() + j * sizeIncrement().height();\n```\n\nNote that while you can set the size increment for all widgets, it only affects windows.\n\nBy default, this property contains a size with zero width and height.\n\n**Warning:** The size increment has no effect under Windows, and may be disregarded by\nthe window manager on X11.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **sizeIncrement**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setSizeIncrement**(*const QSize \\&*) |\n| void | **[setSizeIncrement](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setSizeIncrement-1)**(int *w*, int *h*) |\n\n**See also** [size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop),\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop), and\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeIncrement'
    sizePolicy: QSizePolicy
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### sizePolicy : [QSizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsizepolicy.html)\n\nThis property holds the default layout behavior of the widget\n\nIf there is a [QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html) that manages\nthis widget's children, the size policy specified by that layout is used. If there is no\nsuch [QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html), the result of this\nfunction is used.\n\nThe default policy is Preferred/Preferred, which means that the widget can be freely\nresized, but prefers to be the size\n[sizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint-prop)() returns.\nButton-like widgets set the size policy to specify that they may stretch horizontally,\nbut are fixed vertically. The same applies to lineedit controls (such as\n[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html),\n[QSpinBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qspinbox.html) or an editable\n[QComboBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcombobox.html)) and other horizontally\norientated widgets (such as\n[QProgressBar](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qprogressbar.html)).\n[QToolButton](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtoolbutton.html)'s are normally\nsquare, so they allow growth in both directions. Widgets that support different\ndirections (such as [QSlider](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qslider.html),\n[QScrollBar](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qscrollbar.html) or QHeader) specify\nstretching in the respective direction only. Widgets that can provide scroll bars\n(usually subclasses of\n[QScrollArea](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qscrollarea.html)) tend to specify that\nthey can use additional space, and that they can make do with less than\n[sizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint-prop)().\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSizePolicy | **sizePolicy**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setSizePolicy**(*QSizePolicy*) |\n| void | **[setSizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setSizePolicy-1)**(QSizePolicy::Policy *horizontal*, QSizePolicy::Policy *vertical*) |\n\n**See also**\n[sizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint-prop)(),\n[QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html),\n[QSizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsizepolicy.html), and\n[updateGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#updateGeometry)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizePolicy"
    statusTip: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### statusTip : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's status tip\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty string.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **statusTip**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setStatusTip**(*const QString \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [toolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip-prop) and\n[whatsThis](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#whatsThis-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#statusTip"
    styleSheet: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### styleSheet : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's style sheet\n\nThe style sheet contains a textual description of customizations to the widget's style,\nas described in the [Qt Style\nSheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html) document.\n\nSince Qt 4.5, Qt style sheets fully supports macOS.\n\n**Warning:** Qt style sheets are currently not supported for custom\n[QStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html) subclasses. We plan to address\nthis in some future release.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.2.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **styleSheet**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setStyleSheet**(const QString \\&*styleSheet*) |\n\n**See also** [setStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setStyle)(),\n[QApplication::styleSheet](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#styleSheet-prop),\nand [Qt Style Sheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#styleSheet"
    tabletTracking: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### tabletTracking : bool\n\nThis property holds whether tablet tracking is enabled for the widget\n\nIf tablet tracking is disabled (the default), the widget only receives tablet move\nevents when the stylus is in contact with the tablet, or at least one stylus button is\npressed, while the stylus is being moved.\n\nIf tablet tracking is enabled, the widget receives tablet move events even while\nhovering in proximity. This is useful for monitoring position as well as the auxiliary\nproperties such as rotation and tilt, and providing feedback in the UI.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 5.9.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **hasTabletTracking**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setTabletTracking**(bool *enable*) |\n\n**See also**\n[tabletEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#tabletEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#tabletTracking'
    toolTip: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### toolTip : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's tooltip\n\nNote that by default tooltips are only shown for widgets that are children of the active\nwindow. You can change this behavior by setting the attribute\n[Qt::WA\\_AlwaysShowToolTips](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WidgetAttribute-enum)\non the *window*, not on the widget with the tooltip.\n\nIf you want to control a tooltip's behavior, you can intercept the\n[event](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#event)() function and catch the\n[QEvent::ToolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#Type-enum) event (e.g.,\nif you want to customize the area for which the tooltip should be shown).\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty string.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **toolTip**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setToolTip**(*const QString \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [QToolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtooltip.html),\n[statusTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#statusTip-prop), and\n[whatsThis](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#whatsThis-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip"
    toolTipDuration: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### toolTipDuration : int\n\nThis property holds the widget's tooltip duration\n\nSpecifies how long time the tooltip will be displayed, in milliseconds. If the value is\n-1 (default) the duration is calculated depending on the length of the tooltip.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 5.2.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **toolTipDuration**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setToolTipDuration**(int *msec*) |\n\n**See also** [toolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTipDuration"
    updatesEnabled: bool
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### updatesEnabled : bool\n\nThis property holds whether updates are enabled\n\nAn updates enabled widget receives paint events and has a system background; a disabled\nwidget does not. This also implies that calling\n[update](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#update)() and\n[repaint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#repaint)() has no effect if\nupdates are disabled.\n\nBy default, this property is `true`.\n\nsetUpdatesEnabled() is normally used to disable updates for a short period of time, for\ninstance to avoid screen flicker during large changes. In Qt, widgets normally do not\ngenerate screen flicker, but on X11 the server might erase regions on the screen when\nwidgets get hidden before they can be replaced by other widgets. Disabling updates\nsolves this.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nsetUpdatesEnabled(false);\nbigVisualChanges();\nsetUpdatesEnabled(true);\n```\n\nDisabling a widget implicitly disables all its children. Enabling a widget enables all\nchild widgets *except* top-level widgets or those that have been explicitly disabled.\nRe-enabling updates implicitly calls\n[update](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#update)() on the widget.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **updatesEnabled**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setUpdatesEnabled**(bool *enable*) |\n\n**See also** [paintEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#paintEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#updatesEnabled'
    visible: Any
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### visible : bool\n\nThis property holds whether the widget is visible\n\nCalling setVisible(true) or\n[show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)() sets the widget to\nvisible status if all its parent widgets up to the window are visible. If an ancestor is\nnot visible, the widget won't become visible until all its ancestors are shown. If its\nsize or position has changed, Qt guarantees that a widget gets move and resize events\njust before it is shown. If the widget has not been resized yet, Qt will adjust the\nwidget's size to a useful default using\n[adjustSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#adjustSize)().\n\nCalling setVisible(false) or\n[hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)() hides a widget\nexplicitly. An explicitly hidden widget will never become visible, even if all its\nancestors become visible, unless you show it.\n\nA widget receives show and hide events when its visibility status changes. Between a\nhide and a show event, there is no need to waste CPU cycles preparing or displaying\ninformation to the user. A video application, for example, might simply stop generating\nnew frames.\n\nA widget that happens to be obscured by other windows on the screen is considered to be\nvisible. The same applies to iconified windows and windows that exist on another virtual\ndesktop (on platforms that support this concept). A widget receives spontaneous show and\nhide events when its mapping status is changed by the window system, e.g. a spontaneous\nhide event when the user minimizes the window, and a spontaneous show event when the\nwindow is restored again.\n\nYou almost never have to reimplement the setVisible() function. If you need to change\nsome settings before a widget is shown, use\n[showEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showEvent)() instead. If you\nneed to do some delayed initialization use the Polish event delivered to the\n[event](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#event)() function.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isVisible**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| virtual void | **setVisible**(bool *visible*) |\n\n**See also** [show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)(),\n[hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)(),\n[isHidden](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isHidden)(),\n[isVisibleTo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isVisibleTo)(),\n[isMinimized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimized-prop)(),\n[showEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showEvent)(), and\n[hideEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hideEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#visible"
    whatsThis: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### whatsThis : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's What's This help text.\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty string.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **whatsThis**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWhatsThis**(*const QString \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [QWhatsThis](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwhatsthis.html),\n[QWidget::toolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip-prop), and\n[QWidget::statusTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#statusTip-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#whatsThis"
    width: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### width : const int\n\nThis property holds the width of the widget excluding any window frame\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\n**Note:** Do not use this function to find the width of a screen on a [multiple screen\ndesktop](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdesktopwidget.html). Read [this\nnote](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdesktopwidget.html#screen-geometry) for\ndetails.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **width**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop),\n[height](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#height-prop), and\n[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#width"
    windowFilePath: str
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### windowFilePath : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the file path associated with a widget\n\nThis property only makes sense for windows. It associates a file path with a window. If\nyou set the file path, but have not set the window title, Qt sets the window title to\nthe file name of the specified path, obtained using\n[QFileInfo::fileName](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfileinfo.html#fileName)().\n\nIf the window title is set at any point, then the window title takes precedence and will\nbe shown instead of the file path string.\n\nAdditionally, on macOS, this has an added benefit that it sets the [proxy\nicon](http://developer.apple.com/documentation/UserExperience/Conceptual/OSXHIGuidelines/XHIGWindows/chapter_17_section_3.html)\nfor the window, assuming that the file path exists.\n\nIf no file path is set, this property contains an empty string.\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty string.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.4.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **windowFilePath**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWindowFilePath**(const QString \\&*filePath*) |\n\n**See also**\n[windowTitle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowTitle-prop) and\n[windowIcon](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowIcon-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowFilePath'
    windowIcon: QIcon
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### windowIcon : [QIcon](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qicon.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's icon\n\nThis property only makes sense for windows. If no icon has been set, windowIcon()\nreturns the application icon\n([QApplication::windowIcon](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#windowIcon-prop)()).\n\n**Note:** On macOS, window icons represent the active document, and will not be\ndisplayed unless a file path has also been set using\n[setWindowFilePath](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowFilePath-prop).\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QIcon | **windowIcon**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWindowIcon**(const QIcon \\&*icon*) |\n\n**Notifier signal:**\n\n| void | **[windowIconChanged](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowIconChanged)**(const QIcon \\&*icon*) |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[windowTitle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowTitle-prop) and\n[setWindowFilePath](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowFilePath-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowIcon"
    windowIconText: str
    windowModality: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### windowModality : [Qt::WindowModality](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WindowModality-enum)\n\nThis property holds which windows are blocked by the modal widget\n\nThis property only makes sense for windows. A modal widget prevents widgets in other\nwindows from getting input. The value of this property controls which windows are\nblocked when the widget is visible. Changing this property while the window is visible\nhas no effect; you must [hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)()\nthe widget first, then [show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)() it\nagain.\n\nBy default, this property is\n[Qt::NonModal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WindowModality-enum).\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.1.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| Qt::WindowModality | **windowModality**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWindowModality**(Qt::WindowModality *windowModality*) |\n\n**See also** [isWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isWindow)(),\n[QWidget::modal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#modal-prop), and\n[QDialog](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdialog.html).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowModality'
    windowModified: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### windowModified : bool\n\nThis property holds whether the document shown in the window has unsaved changes\n\nA modified window is a window whose content has changed but has not been saved to disk.\nThis flag will have different effects varied by the platform. On macOS the close button\nwill have a modified look; on other platforms, the window title will have an \'\\*\'\n(asterisk).\n\nThe window title must contain a "\\[\\*\\]" placeholder, which indicates where the \'\\*\'\nshould appear. Normally, it should appear right after the file name (e.g.,\n"document1.txt\\[\\*\\] \\- Text Editor"). If the window isn\'t modified, the placeholder is\nsimply removed.\n\nNote that if a widget is set as modified, all its ancestors will also be set as\nmodified. However, if you call `setWindowModified(false)` on a widget, this will not\npropagate to its parent because other children of the parent might have been modified.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isWindowModified**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWindowModified**(*bool*) |\n\n**See also**\n[windowTitle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowTitle-prop),\n[Application\nExample](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtwidgets-mainwindows-application-example.html),\n[SDI\nExample](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtwidgets-mainwindows-sdi-example.html), and\n[MDI\nExample](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtwidgets-mainwindows-mdi-example.html).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowModified'
    windowOpacity: float
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### windowOpacity : double\n\nThis property holds the level of opacity for the window.\n\nThe valid range of opacity is from 1.0 (completely opaque) to 0.0 (completely\ntransparent).\n\nBy default the value of this property is 1.0.\n\nThis feature is available on Embedded Linux, macOS, Windows, and X11 platforms that\nsupport the Composite extension.\n\n**Note:** On X11 you need to have a composite manager running, and the X11 specific\n\\_NET\\_WM\\_WINDOW\\_OPACITY atom needs to be supported by the window manager you are\nusing.\n\n**Warning:** Changing this property from opaque to transparent might issue a paint event\nthat needs to be processed before the window is displayed correctly. This affects mainly\nthe use of\n[QScreen::grabWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qscreen.html#grabWindow)().\nAlso note that semi-transparent windows update and resize significantly slower than\nopaque windows.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| qreal | **windowOpacity**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWindowOpacity**(qreal *level*) |\n\n**See also** [setMask](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setMask)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowOpacity'
    windowTitle: str
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### windowTitle : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the window title (caption)\n\nThis property only makes sense for top-level widgets, such as windows and dialogs. If no\ncaption has been set, the title is based of the\n[windowFilePath](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowFilePath-prop).\nIf neither of these is set, then the title is an empty string.\n\nIf you use the\n[windowModified](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowModified-prop)\nmechanism, the window title must contain a "\\[\\*\\]" placeholder, which indicates where\nthe \'\\*\' should appear. Normally, it should appear right after the file name (e.g.,\n"document1.txt\\[\\*\\] \\- Text Editor"). If the\n[windowModified](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowModified-prop)\nproperty is `false` (the default), the placeholder is simply removed.\n\nOn some desktop platforms (including Windows and Unix), the application name (from\n[QGuiApplication::applicationDisplayName](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qguiapplication.html#applicationDisplayName-prop))\nis added at the end of the window title, if set. This is done by the QPA plugin, so it\nis shown to the user, but isn\'t part of the windowTitle string.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **windowTitle**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWindowTitle**(*const QString \\&*) |\n\n**Notifier signal:**\n\n| void | **[windowTitleChanged](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowTitleChanged)**(const QString \\&*title*) |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also**\n[windowIcon](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowIcon-prop),\n[windowModified](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowModified-prop),\nand\n[windowFilePath](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowFilePath-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#windowTitle'
    x: int
