class QWizard:
    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

A wizard (also called an assistant on macOS) is a special type of input dialog that
consists of a sequence of pages. A wizard's purpose is to guide the user through a
process step by step. Wizards are useful for complex or infrequent tasks that users may
find difficult to learn.

QWizard inherits [QDialog](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdialog.html) and
represents a wizard. Each page is a
[QWizardPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html) (a
[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html) subclass). To create your own
wizards, you can use these classes directly, or you can subclass them for more control.

Topics:

### A Trivial Example

The following example illustrates how to create wizard pages and add them to a wizard.
For more advanced examples, see [Class
Wizard](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtwidgets-dialogs-classwizard-example.html)
and [License
Wizard](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtwidgets-dialogs-licensewizard-example.html).

```
QWizardPage *createIntroPage()
{
    QWizardPage *page = new QWizardPage;
    page->setTitle("Introduction");

    QLabel *label = new QLabel("This wizard will help you register your copy "
                               "of Super Product Two.");
    label->setWordWrap(true);

    QVBoxLayout *layout = new QVBoxLayout;
    layout->addWidget(label);
    page->setLayout(layout);

    return page;
}

QWizardPage *createRegistrationPage()
{
    ...
}

QWizardPage *createConclusionPage()
{
    ...
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    QCoreApplication::setAttribute(Qt::AA_UseHighDpiPixmaps);

    QApplication app(argc, argv);

#ifndef QT_NO_TRANSLATION
    QString translatorFileName = QLatin1String("qtbase_");
    translatorFileName += QLocale::system().name();
    QTranslator *translator = new QTranslator(&app);
    if (translator->load(translatorFileName, QLibraryInfo::location(QLibraryInfo::TranslationsPath)))
        app.installTranslator(translator);
#endif

    QWizard wizard;
    wizard.addPage(createIntroPage());
    wizard.addPage(createRegistrationPage());
    wizard.addPage(createConclusionPage());

    wizard.setWindowTitle("Trivial Wizard");
    wizard.show();

    return app.exec();
}
```

### Wizard Look and Feel

QWizard supports four wizard looks:

- [ClassicStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum)
- [ModernStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum)
- [MacStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum)
- [AeroStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum)

You can explicitly set the look to use using
[setWizardStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#wizardStyle-prop)()
(e.g., if you want the same look on all platforms).

| [ClassicStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) | [ModernStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) | [MacStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) | [AeroStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
|  |  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  |

Note: [AeroStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) has
effect only on a Windows Vista system with alpha compositing enabled.
[ModernStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) is used
as a fallback when this condition is not met.

In addition to the wizard style, there are several options that control the look and
feel of the wizard. These can be set using
[setOption](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#setOption)() or
[setOptions](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#options-prop)(). For
example,
[HaveHelpButton](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardOption-enum)
makes QWizard show a **Help** button along with the other wizard buttons.

You can even change the order of the wizard buttons to any arbitrary order using
[setButtonLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#setButtonLayout)(),
and you can add up to three custom buttons (e.g., a **Print** button) to the button row.
This is achieved by calling
[setButton](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#setButton)() or
[setButtonText](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#setButtonText)() with
[CustomButton1](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardButton-enum),
[CustomButton2](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardButton-enum), or
[CustomButton3](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardButton-enum) to
set up the button, and by enabling the
[HaveCustomButton1](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardOption-enum),
[HaveCustomButton2](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardOption-enum),
or
[HaveCustomButton3](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardOption-enum)
options. Whenever the user clicks a custom button,
[customButtonClicked](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#customButtonClicked)()
is emitted. For example:

```
        wizard()->setButtonText(QWizard::CustomButton1, tr("&Print"));
        wizard()->setOption(QWizard::HaveCustomButton1, true);
        connect(wizard(), &QWizard::customButtonClicked,
                this, &ConclusionPage::printButtonClicked);
```

### Elements of a Wizard Page

Wizards consist of a sequence of
[QWizardPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html)s. At any time, only
one page is shown. A page has the following attributes:

- A [title](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#title-prop).
- A [subTitle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#subTitle-prop).
- A set of pixmaps, which may or may not be honored, depending on the wizard's style:
  - [WatermarkPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) (used by [ClassicStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) and [ModernStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum))
  - [BannerPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) (used by [ModernStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum))
  - [LogoPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) (used by [ClassicStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) and [ModernStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum))
  - [BackgroundPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) (used by [MacStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum))

The diagram belows shows how QWizard renders these attributes, assuming they are all
present and
[ModernStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum) is used:

![](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/images/qtwizard-nonmacpage.png)

When a [subTitle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#subTitle-prop) is
set, QWizard displays it in a header, in which case it also uses the
[BannerPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) and
the [LogoPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) to
decorate the header. The
[WatermarkPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) is
displayed on the left side, below the header. At the bottom, there is a row of buttons
allowing the user to navigate through the pages.

The page itself (the [QWizardPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html)
widget) occupies the area between the header, the watermark, and the button row.
Typically, the page is a
[QWizardPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html) on which a
[QGridLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html) is installed, with
standard child widgets ([QLabel](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlabel.html)s,
[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html)s, etc.).

If the wizard's style is
[MacStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum), the page
looks radically different:

![](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/images/qtwizard-macpage.png)

The watermark, banner, and logo pixmaps are ignored by the
[MacStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardStyle-enum). If the
[BackgroundPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardPixmap-enum) is
set, it is used as the background for the wizard; otherwise, a default "assistant" image
is used.

The title and subtitle are set by calling
[QWizardPage::setTitle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#title-prop)()
and
[QWizardPage::setSubTitle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#subTitle-prop)()
on the individual pages. They may be plain text or HTML (see
[titleFormat](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#titleFormat-prop) and
[subTitleFormat](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#subTitleFormat-prop)).
The pixmaps can be set globally for the entire wizard using
[setPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#setPixmap)(), or on a
per-page basis using
[QWizardPage::setPixmap](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#setPixmap)().

### Registering and Using Fields

In many wizards, the contents of a page may affect the default values of the fields of a
later page. To make it easy to communicate between pages, QWizard supports a "field"
mechanism that allows you to register a field (e.g., a
[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html)) on a page and to access
its value from any page. It is also possible to specify mandatory fields (i.e., fields
that must be filled before the user can advance to the next page).

To register a field, call
[QWizardPage::registerField](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#registerField)()
field. For example:

```
ClassInfoPage::ClassInfoPage(QWidget *parent)
    : QWizardPage(parent)
{
    ...
    classNameLabel = new QLabel(tr("&Class name:"));
    classNameLineEdit = new QLineEdit;
    classNameLabel->setBuddy(classNameLineEdit);

    baseClassLabel = new QLabel(tr("B&ase class:"));
    baseClassLineEdit = new QLineEdit;
    baseClassLabel->setBuddy(baseClassLineEdit);

    qobjectMacroCheckBox = new QCheckBox(tr("Generate Q_OBJECT &macro"));

    registerField("className*", classNameLineEdit);
    registerField("baseClass", baseClassLineEdit);
    registerField("qobjectMacro", qobjectMacroCheckBox);
    ...
}
```

The above code registers three fields, `className`, `baseClass`, and `qobjectMacro`,
which are associated with three child widgets. The asterisk (`*`) next to `className`
denotes a mandatory field.

The fields of any page are accessible from any other page. For example:

```
void OutputFilesPage::initializePage()
{
    QString className = field("className").toString();
    headerLineEdit->setText(className.toLower() + ".h");
    implementationLineEdit->setText(className.toLower() + ".cpp");
    outputDirLineEdit->setText(QDir::toNativeSeparators(QDir::tempPath()));
}
```

Here, we call
[QWizardPage::field](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#field)() to
access the contents of the `className` field (which was defined in the `ClassInfoPage`)
and use it to initialize the `OutputFilePage`. The field's contents is returned as a
[QVariant](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qvariant.html).

When we create a field using
[QWizardPage::registerField](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#registerField)(),
we pass a unique field name and a widget. We can also provide a Qt property name and a
"changed" signal (a signal that is emitted when the property changes) as third and
fourth arguments; however, this is not necessary for the most common Qt widgets, such as
[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html),
[QCheckBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcheckbox.html), and
[QComboBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcombobox.html), because QWizard knows
which properties to look for.

If an asterisk (`*`) is appended to the name when the property is registered, the field
is a *mandatory field*. When a page has mandatory fields, the **Next** and/or **Finish**
buttons are enabled only when all mandatory fields are filled.

To consider a field "filled", QWizard simply checks that the field's current value
doesn't equal the original value (the value it had when
[initializePage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#initializePage)() was
called). For [QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html) and
[QAbstractSpinBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qabstractspinbox.html) subclasses,
QWizard also checks that
[hasAcceptableInput()](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html#acceptableInput-prop)
returns true, to honor any validator or mask.

QWizard's mandatory field mechanism is provided for convenience. A more powerful (but
also more cumbersome) alternative is to reimplement
[QWizardPage::isComplete](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#isComplete)()
and to emit the
[QWizardPage::completeChanged](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#completeChanged)()
signal whenever the page becomes complete or incomplete.

The enabled/disabled state of the **Next** and/or **Finish** buttons is one way to
perform validation on the user input. Another way is to reimplement
[validateCurrentPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#validateCurrentPage)()
(or
[QWizardPage::validatePage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#validatePage)())
to perform some last-minute validation (and show an error message if the user has
entered incomplete or invalid information). If the function returns `true`, the next
page is shown (or the wizard finishes); otherwise, the current page stays up.

### Creating Linear Wizards

Most wizards have a linear structure, with page 1 followed by page 2 and so on until the
last page. The [Class
Wizard](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtwidgets-dialogs-classwizard-example.html)
example is such a wizard. With QWizard, linear wizards are created by instantiating the
[QWizardPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html)s and inserting them
using [addPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#addPage)(). By default,
the pages are shown in the order in which they were added. For example:

```
ClassWizard::ClassWizard(QWidget *parent)
    : QWizard(parent)
{
    addPage(new IntroPage);
    addPage(new ClassInfoPage);
    addPage(new CodeStylePage);
    addPage(new OutputFilesPage);
    addPage(new ConclusionPage);
    ...
}
```

When a page is about to be shown, QWizard calls
[initializePage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#initializePage)()
(which in turn calls
[QWizardPage::initializePage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#initializePage)())
to fill the page with default values. By default, this function does nothing, but it can
be reimplemented to initialize the page's contents based on other pages' fields (see the
[example above](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#initialize-page)).

If the user presses **Back**,
[cleanupPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#cleanupPage)() is called
(which in turn calls
[QWizardPage::cleanupPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#cleanupPage)()).
The default implementation resets the page's fields to their original values (the values
they had before
[initializePage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#initializePage)() was
called). If you want the **Back** button to be non-destructive and keep the values
entered by the user, simply enable the
[IndependentPages](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#WizardOption-enum)
option.

### Creating Non-Linear Wizards

Some wizards are more complex in that they allow different traversal paths based on the
information provided by the user. The [License
Wizard](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtwidgets-dialogs-licensewizard-example.html)
example illustrates this. It provides five wizard pages; depending on which options are
selected, the user can reach different pages.

![](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/images/licensewizard-flow.png)

In complex wizards, pages are identified by IDs. These IDs are typically defined using
an enum. For example:

```
class LicenseWizard : public QWizard
{
    ...
    enum { Page_Intro, Page_Evaluate, Page_Register, Page_Details,
           Page_Conclusion };
    ...
};
```

The pages are inserted using
[setPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#setPage)(), which takes an ID
and an instance of [QWizardPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html)
(or of a subclass):

```
LicenseWizard::LicenseWizard(QWidget *parent)
    : QWizard(parent)
{
    setPage(Page_Intro, new IntroPage);
    setPage(Page_Evaluate, new EvaluatePage);
    setPage(Page_Register, new RegisterPage);
    setPage(Page_Details, new DetailsPage);
    setPage(Page_Conclusion, new ConclusionPage);
    ...
}
```

By default, the pages are shown in increasing ID order. To provide a dynamic order that
depends on the options chosen by the user, we must reimplement
[QWizardPage::nextId](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizardpage.html#nextId)(). For
example:

```
int IntroPage::nextId() const
{
    if (evaluateRadioButton->isChecked()) {
        return LicenseWizard::Page_Evaluate;
    } else {
        return LicenseWizard::Page_Register;
    }
}

int EvaluatePage::nextId() const
{
    return LicenseWizard::Page_Conclusion;
}

int RegisterPage::nextId() const
{
    if (upgradeKeyLineEdit->text().isEmpty()) {
        return LicenseWizard::Page_Details;
    } else {
        return LicenseWizard::Page_Conclusion;
    }
}

int DetailsPage::nextId() const
{
    return LicenseWizard::Page_Conclusion;
}

int ConclusionPage::nextId() const
{
    return -1;
}
```

It would also be possible to put all the logic in one place, in a
[QWizard::nextId](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#nextId)()
reimplementation. For example:

```
int LicenseWizard::nextId() const
{
    switch (currentId()) {
    case Page_Intro:
        if (field("intro.evaluate").toBool()) {
            return Page_Evaluate;
        } else {
            return Page_Register;
        }
    case Page_Evaluate:
        return Page_Conclusion;
    case Page_Register:
        if (field("register.upgradeKey").toString().isEmpty()) {
            return Page_Details;
        } else {
            return Page_Conclusion;
        }
    case Page_Details:
        return Page_Conclusion;
    case Page_Conclusion:
    default:
        return -1;
    }
}
```

To start at another page than the page with the lowest ID, call
[setStartId](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#startId-prop)().

To test whether a page has been visited or not, call
[hasVisitedPage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwizard.html#hasVisitedPage)(). For
example:

```
void ConclusionPage::initializePage()
{
    QString licenseText;

    if (wizard()->hasVisitedPage(LicenseWizard::Page_Evaluate)) {
        licenseText = tr("<u>Evaluation License Agreement:</u> "
                         "You can use this software for 30 days and make one "
                         "backup, but you are not allowed to distribute it.");
    } else if (wizard()->hasVisitedPage(LicenseWizard::Page_Details)) {
        licenseText = tr("<u>First-Time License Agreement:</u> "
                         "You can use this software subject to the license "
                         "you will receive by email.");
    } else {
        licenseText = tr("<u>Upgrade License Agreement:</u> "
                         "This software is licensed under the terms of your "
                         "current license.");
    }
    bottomLabel->setText(licenseText);
}
```

